Q: Append items to an array in an object in Swift A: <p><sub>If your minimum target starts from iOS 17 or higher, you can use <code>@Observable</code> as <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79989758/5623035">Leo already responded</a>.</sub></p>
<p>If you are supporting iOS 13 or higher, you should get some help from the Combine framework.</p>
<ol>
<li>import <code>Combine</code></li>
<li>Inherit from <code>ObservableObject</code></li>
<li>Use <code>@Published</code> property wrapper on the <code>var</code></li>
<li>Use <code>@StateObject</code> at the call-side (or <code>@ObservedObject</code> if you manage to pass the object instead of making it directly inside the view).</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>import Combine // 👈 1. 

//                      👇 2.
class TestObject: ObservableObject {
    // 👇 3.
    @Published var items: [String] = [&quot;item 1&quot;]
}

struct ContentView: View {
    //  👇 4.
    @StateObject var test = TestObject()
    @State var c: Int = 1

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            List(test.items, id: \.self){
                Text($0)
            }
            Button(&quot;Add&quot;) {
                c += 1;
                test.items.append(&quot;item &quot; + String(c))
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>⚠️ Note that either way, there is absolutely no need to inherit from <code>NSObject</code>.</p>

